    "year": 2021,
    "source": "OECD",
    "sourceUrl": "https://data-explorer.oecd.org/vis?df[ds]=DisseminateFinalDMZ&df[id]=DSD_REG_ECO%40DF_INC",
    "extractedAt": "2026-10-03",
    "unit": "usd_ppp_per_capita",
    "dimensions": [
      "value"
    ],
    "methodNote": "Net disposable household income per capita, purchasing-power-parity-adjusted US dollars, per NUTS 2 region, OECD's Regional Statistics database (DSD_REG_ECO@DF_INC, MEASURE=B6N, PRICES=V, UNIT_MEASURE=USD_PPP_PS), 2021 — the newest year before a real coverage drop. Deliberately not GDP per capita: GDP is production-based and can be skewed by corporate activity concentrated in a region (this map's own GDP layer documents Ireland's and Luxembourg's tax-accounting outliers for exactly this reason); disposable income is what households actually have after taxes and transfers, the more relevant figure for 'what does a euro/dollar of income here actually buy a resident.'",
    "grain": "nuts2",
    "kind": "scalar",
    "coverageNote": "Albania has ZERO codes (not an OECD member). The UK and France both report real data but at a coarser, NUTS-1-shaped grain than this map's NUTS 2 boundaries, so ZERO codes from either match — the same shape confirmed independently across every NUTS 2 layer in this batch except rooms-per-person, where France is absent entirely rather than coarse-grained."
